@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:architecture] android-version-has-changelog
Every change to `versionName` in `app/build.gradle.kts` MUST come with a `## <version>`
section at the top of `android/CHANGELOG.md`: a summary sentence written for the reader
and at least one bullet, newest first. The update screen, What's new and `latest.json`
read their words from it, never from commit messages (DESIGN.md rule 104, changelog check).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security;architecture] android-version-code-rises
Every published build MUST have a higher `versionCode` than the one before it, so an
installed phone sees it as newer and Android accepts it as an update.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:security] android-pinned-signing-key
The published APK MUST be signed with the pinned `app/debug.keystore`, so every machine
signs identically and an installed app keeps accepting updates. The key MUST NOT change.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design;architecture] android-design-md-is-the-spec
`android/DESIGN.md` is the spec for how the app looks and moves. Its front matter is the
source of truth for tokens, and its numbered rules are cited by number. A change that
departs from a rule changes the rule in the same pull request, with its source.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design;architecture] android-design-checks-never-waived
`pnpm run android:design` MUST pass in CI. A failing check is fixed, never skipped, and a
baseline MAY only go down: a new raw value or banned use is replaced with a token or a kit
part, never added to the baseline (DESIGN.md Enforcement).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-tokens-only
A composable MUST NOT write a raw hex colour, font size or corner radius. Colours come
from `LocalPullsColors`, type from `PullsType`, radii and spacing from `PullsRadius` and
`PullsSpace` (DESIGN.md Do's and Don'ts; raw-values check).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design;architecture] android-one-component-per-pattern
One pattern, one component, used everywhere: a fold heading, a sheet, a sheet row, a
search field, a chip, a segmented control, a close button, a row swipe, a toast. A screen
MUST reuse the kit's part and MUST NOT build its own copy of a pattern the app already
has ("Collapsible sections differ ... one component, used everywhere", J18; rules 49,
70, 119, 120).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-one-fold-heading
Every heading that folds rows away is `GroupHeading`: the name, the count, a chevron that
turns as it folds, a line under it while folded, and the rows leaving and returning with
motion. The Inbox, Tours, the Overview's file sections, the Files sheet and any new
folding list use it (rule 119; recording 15 Oct 12:39, J18).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-one-dialect
Every screen is in the mobile style of `ui/design/Kit.kt` and the `Lu` icons. The
web-port kit (`Components.kt`, Phosphor icons) is frozen: new code MUST NOT use it, and
its count MAY only fall (rule 49).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-kit-over-material
Where the kit has a component, a screen MUST NOT use Material's: no Material buttons,
switches, checkboxes, text fields, dialogs, tabs, cards, dividers, progress indicators,
FABs, icons or `ModalBottomSheet` (bans check; rules 37, 48, 73).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-both-themes
A screen is not done until it is right in light and in dark. Every colour token has a
dark pair, and dark surfaces are a neutral grey with no colour cast (rule 76; F13).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;design] android-information-first
Every element on a screen carries a fact or does something; anything that carries
neither is cut. No initial avatars, decorative glyphs, FAB or Share (rule 1).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-not-wanted
The app MUST NOT have voice input or dictation, a suggestion mode, Share on a pull
request or tour, "Up next" inside a tour, a floating action button, "Later" as a row
action, numbered step pips or step counters in progress (rule 11).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;design] android-no-explaining-lines
A control MUST NOT carry a line of text that explains it ("The Analyze button runs the
last choice", "Shift-click to select a range", the server and update notes under
Settings, the lines under the sounds). If a control needs explaining, the control changes
(rule 87; F25, F30, H8, J4).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-no-tiny-text
Text is never below 15sp and never uppercase or tracked; a section label is one word. The
only exception is the footnote under a model list, which Tavi asked to be tiny (rules 14
and 89).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;design] android-whole-identifiers
Titles of pull requests, commits, tours and files wrap whole and are never cut with an
ellipsis. Names (an author, a branch) keep to one line and give way only at their end,
and a path is cut from the left by whole folders (rules 2, 90 and 111; F23, F24, H15).
The one exception is a pull request's title pinned at the top of its scrolled Overview,
which is one line and MAY end in an ellipsis (recording of 16 Oct 12:10, 20:34 to 21:03;
N38, Tavi's decision).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-no-loading-screens
No page or list shows a loading screen, a spinner page or "Loading..." text. What the app
already knows shows in the first frame, and only content still on its way stands in as a
shimmering skeleton of its shape (rules 50, 51 and 85).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-refresh-is-silent
There is no Refresh button, command or pull-to-refresh indicator anywhere. The app
refreshes itself: new commits on an open pull request arrive without a tap, and a page
reloads quietly when the app comes back to the front ("we should be refreshing ourselves",
rule 52; F17).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design;product] android-no-link-out-icons
No screen shows an arrow leaving a box or any other "opens elsewhere" mark: "We're not a
web app. We don't open links, not like this." A row that goes to GitHub carries GitHub's
own mark; any other row says in words what it does (rule 100).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-launcher-icon-torch
The launcher icon is "Torch", which Tavi picked on 15 Oct 2026 from the Pulls Icon Ideas
canvas: a nine by nine grid of days in GitHub's dark greens, with a beam of light from the
bottom left corner lighting a wedge of them. It is flat: solid fills only, no gradients,
shadows or highlights ("3d look is lame"). The themed icon and the notification icon are the
beam alone. It is drawn only by `android/scripts/gen-icon.mjs`; the drawables are its output.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-no-ai-icons
No icon marks AI: no sparkle, robot head, brain or compass. AI actions are marked by their
words and by the live indicator. The only marks are the Claude Code and Codex logos, in
colour, and the chosen model's own icon (rules 37 and 48).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-tappable-does-something
Anything that looks tappable does something when tapped, and anything that does nothing
does not light up when pressed (rules 53 and 82; F36).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-irreversible-never-primary
The main action at the bottom of a screen is the reviewing action (Review, Next). An
action that cannot be undone is never the main action and is never one tap away (rules 7
and 116).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;security] android-risky-actions-ask-first
An action that changes the pull request for other people or cannot be undone (approve,
request changes, merge, auto-merge on, update branch, draft, ready, close, reopen) MUST
open a page that says what will happen, with the one button that does it. Turning off
something that only stops a future action MAY skip the page ("you should double check
that I actually meant to use that", rule 116; J29).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;security] android-only-what-you-may-do
A control for something the person cannot do on GitHub is not shown: no edit field on a
title they cannot edit, no Archive on a pull request that is not theirs (recording
15 Oct 13:54, 09:42 to 10:42; K20, K21).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-no-dead-ends
No state is a dead end. A first tap answers at once, a second quick tap never cancels
work, and a cancelled or failed run always offers a way to start again (rule 63).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:motion] android-motion-follows-the-gesture
Everything moves the way the person moved it: what a finger drags follows the finger, and
when let go it settles back or carries on the way it went, never springing back toward
the start first. The next page comes from the right, the previous from the left, and Back
always goes backward (rule 105).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:motion] android-reactive-first
A control or sheet answers the instant it is touched. A spring or bounce is a bonus, and
a plain change that starts at once beats a springy one that starts late (rule 68).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:motion;product] android-respects-remove-animations
With the system setting "Remove animations" on, looping effects, page and tab switches,
sheets, menus, icon swaps, the thinking shimmer and the theme wipe hold still or change at
once (rule 73).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;design] android-same-thing-same-feel
The same kind of control sounds and vibrates the same on every page. A screen MUST NOT
choose its own cue or vibration for a control the kit already gives one ("You cannot use
a different vibration and sound for the same element on different pages", rule 120; J20).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-shows-what-it-has
What one screen has already loaded is never fetched again before another shows it: the
profile photo, a pull request's title, branches, size and author from the row it was
opened from (rule 128; J8, J13, H29).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-fits-the-phone
Nothing overflows a 390 by 844 frame: no horizontal page scroll, no scrollbar, nothing
inside the 16dp margin except full-bleed bands, and sliding rows clip inside the frame
(rule 25).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design;architecture] android-component-has-book-page
A new shared component in `ui/design` lands with its UI Book page in `book.json`, its
previews and, when DESIGN.md sizes it, a specimen, in the same change (DESIGN.md
Components; book check).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-words-name-their-effect
A button names its effect in words a GitHub reviewer would use, on one line: a wrapped
button label is a defect ("Next unreviewed" becomes "Next"). Every paragraph of text is
labelled (Summary, Description) (rules 6 and 24).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-no-number-cards
Counts are never stat tiles, KPI cards or boxed mini-widgets, and never a run-on sentence
of counts; they sit in aligned label and value columns that do not move when a digit
changes (rules 8 and 22).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-meta-lines-are-one-line
A meta line (author · repository #number · age; head → base) stays on one line, each
segment giving way at its end rather than wrapping, and its variable fields have a fixed
width (rules 3 and 26).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-status-words-where-they-vary
A status word appears only where it varies: a group titled "Ready to read" does not
repeat Ready on each row, and failing or passing gets a real icon, not a dot (rule 16).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-no-copy-under-lists
A populated list has no explanatory copy or empty-state prose under it, and a tappable
row looks tappable (rule 4).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:motion;design] android-shared-things-stay-put
When a screen changes state, whatever is present in both states stays exactly where it
was, so the change reads as a layer arriving (rule 69).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;design] android-working-cards-keep-their-height
Nothing on a page moves while AI works: a card that shows running work is one fixed
height in every state, its status one line ending in an ellipsis, with more one tap away
(rule 86; F37).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-one-job-one-control
Each job has one control: no second Stop beside the orb, a repeat action is the
try-again arrow with an accessibility label rather than the word Redo (rule 87; F35,
F39).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-sheets-over-pages
Grouped actions, chat, the composer and files open as bottom sheets over the page, not
as new pages (rule 5).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:design] android-measured-not-eyeballed
A screen is built to the design canvas measurably: render it beside the canvas row at
the same size and measure the gaps; "close" is not accepted and "different" is a defect
(rule 47).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-liked-things-stay
What Tavi said he liked MUST NOT regress without his word: pull down for Commands and its
search, the profile sheet, Settings, sheets at their normal height, the scope chips
rearranging, the Files list, the description on the Overview, the Diff, mark reviewed
then the next file, the Tour's sections and Glossary, the "Look for problems" row, and
the sheet and text-fade transitions (DESIGN.md Keep as is; recording 15 Oct 13:54,
00:45 and 10:16).

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product] android-github-links-open-in-pulls
Any GitHub pull request link MUST open in Pulls from the three ways a phone hands one
over: a tapped link (after Settings, GitHub links allows it), Share, and selected text's
Open in Pulls. The shared text only has to contain the link. A link that arrives while
Pulls is open MUST open over the page on screen, so Back returns there, and text Pulls
cannot open MUST say so. Issue, code and other GitHub pages MUST stay in the browser
(Tavi, 16 Oct: "Open link in Pulls or Share with Pulls for any GitHub links on my phone").
